 {:seon.task/name "history-steps-hold-below-one-step"
  :seon.task/plan "From the branch review of jolt/agent-conversation (2026-10-25, root's scratchpad branch-review.md, follow-ups): `seon.context/kept` raises each boundary to a multiple of :seon.config.render/history-step but clamps it to the newest record, so while a run has no more records than one step and its history is over the budget, the boundary moves every turn and the prefix the provider caches breaks each turn. Correct: below one step the boundary holds as above it. Proof: a kept case of fewer parts than the step, over the budget, whose picks hold for two growing histories. Done otherwise (2026-10-25, worktree the-history-cut-holds-between-jumps): the step is gone. A resumed run (a-map #2, 35-39k tokens a request, past the 32k budget) recorded requests that shared only their first two parts (962 tokens) on 22 of 28 turns: the two boundaries, recomputed each turn, moved back and forth (e23, e39, e7, e55 ...), and the provider served 23% of 1.1M tokens from its cache. `seon.context/kept` now walks the parts oldest first and holds its two cuts, advancing them only when what is sent passes the budget, to half of it, so between jumps each request is the one before and what was added; the left-out note moved from last to just after the task's data (sent last, it broke every prefix by one message). Re-rendered offline from that run's records (the scratch paper measure/history-cut-rerender.clj (git history)): the share of each request shared with the one before 22.5% -> 93.7%, 23 breaks -> 1 in 29 turns, tokens a turn 35.1k -> 27.3k. :seon.config.render/history-step deleted."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-debug-read-back-prints-with-its-turns-config"
  :seon.task/plan "From the branch review of jolt/agent-conversation (2026-10-25, root's scratchpad branch-review.md, follow-ups): `seon.context/parts-of` prints a turn's records with the config in force now (budgets through cfg/value), so it equals what the turn sent only while the config is unchanged. Correct: the read-back prints with the config as of the turn (seon.config/from-db as of the turn's commit). Proof: a turn read back after a budget changes equals what it sent."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "the-screen-shows-the-sections-nearest-the-plan"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-10-25: embed the docs and pull in related concepts that we can refine over the session. What exists: every wiki section has a vector on main (docs/vector.md#what-is-embedded, `{:of :seon.doc/address}` restricts ```seon.vector/near``` to sections), the plan has a vector, and the task's data shows only the sections the plan cites (```seon.context/task-data``` :sections). A one-line request cites nothing, so its agent starts with no section and no write set (dogfood, the archived paper dogfood-and-docs-2026-10-25.md (git history) gap 3: about ten evals each of hand exploration). The change: the turn's part (the screen, made again each turn, so it follows the plan as the agent rewrites it; the run's part stays fixed for the prompt cache) shows :related, the k sections nearest the plan's current vector that the plan does not cite, each at summary depth through ```seon.doc/view``` (address and first sentence), with the form that reads one whole (`(my.agent/doc \"docs/page.md#anchor\")` once `seon.doc/a-section-reads-at-each-level` merges, else `seon.doc/view`). k is a config key read through the one resolver like `:seon.config.render/similar` (a new key: name it, default, unit and what 0 means, and say why `:seon.config.render/similar` cannot serve; that is the owner's gate, approved in principle by the request above). Computed only when the plan's vector changed (the render cache keyed by its reads: an unchanged plan costs nothing). Measure on the measured requests (`seon.fixtures/requests`, scripted provider, no paid calls): which related sections a scripted agent reads, and tokens per turn; then a paid comparison waits for root's go. Decided (2026-10-25, this worktree): the key is :seon.config.render/related, unit sections, default 5, 0 shows none; :seon.config.render/similar cannot serve: it counts program functions, in the task's data (the run's fixed part, for the prompt cache), and is off by step 6's measurement, while these are sections on the screen that follow the plan. ```seon.context/related``` ranks the sections and ```seon.context/related-at``` reuses its answer while the count and the branch's last vector write are the same. The owner, 2026-10-25, on the first version (an entry of my.task/status): \"I want everything to be explicit evals in our dynamic eval so the embedding references can be dynamic and refined as the session goes on\", and enough shown that agents dig for more: so it is ```my.task/related```, a form of ```seon.screen/forms``` (recorded as an eval each turn), and ```my.agent/related``` for the agent's own text or a var and a count (the screen form asks no effect, so no test reaching the screen reaches a process), each line at summary depth with the forms that read one whole and search further (```seon.context/related-view```); the read form is seon.doc/view until my.agent/help reads any address."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/the-related-part-shows-the-sections-nearest-the-plan seon.context-test/related-sections-follow-a-rewritten-plan seon.context-cost-test/related-sections-cost-nothing-when-the-plan-is-unchanged}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-task-pulls-what-agents-read"
  :seon.task/plan "Kept, not merged (root, 2026-10-25): the pulls are built on their own branch, behind :seon.config.render/pulls (off by default), and come back when the pipeline pilot can measure turns to green with them on against off (AGENTS.md section 3: a key nothing measured has asked for waits). What they pull into the task's data (seon.context/task-data), from what agents read by eval that the task's data could have shown (measured 2026-10-25 on the 8 runs whose eval forms survive, 143 reads: 12% already shown, about a third pullable): :test-ns, each proof namespace's ns form and helpers a line each (the proof's test namespace was read in 7 of 8 runs); :functions, the my.* functions the system prompt names in its code spans (15 source reads of my.task/status); :seeded n, a design plan citing no var gets the n functions nearest it (dogfood: about ten evals of hand exploration each); :call-sites, each caller's call (read in its namespace, the head resolved by ns-resolve, only where :form/refs name the var); :callees, each write-set var's program callees a line each; :example-test, one test calling it that is not a proof. Measured cost on the branch (estimated tokens, chars/4 of the uncut task data, before the printer's budget), on a task citing 8 vars (task data 5,821) and one citing 1 (1,778): :test-ns +1,079 / +1,587, :functions +191, :call-sites +971 / +30, :callees +2,508 / +479 (ten times its 240 estimate on a wide write set: cut to the k nearest, or a count), :example-test +805 / +182; :seeded proven, not measured on a real plan. Its self-review rated it overbuilt as long as nothing measures it. The pilot measures: the measured requests (seon.fixtures/requests), each pull on against all off, turns to green and tokens per turn; keep a pull only where turns fall."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/the-task-pulls-what-its-config-names}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-request-is-finished-from-its-page-in-about-twenty-turns" :seon.task/plan "The free context audit's gate before any paid run (owner: \"Only use paid runs though when we legitimately have solved the known problems with our context ... fix the problems ... at the root\"). The known problems P1-P9: the archived paper known-context-problems-2026-10-26.md (git history); audit 1: the archived paper context-audit-1-2026-10-26.md (git history); audit 2 (main 735daa366): the archived paper context-audit-2-2026-10-27.md (git history), NOT CLEAN: yes help, bytes; likely related-list, message, first-sentence, cut-char, star; at risk mixed, namespaced; no long-string. Audit 3's prerequisites, each on main first: agent view: a cited var's source and its tests drawn as separate children under their own budgets (`seon.render.ui/cited`; the source starved print-value's 27 tests), the related part drawn through related-view's lines not bare addresses (`seon.render.ui/related`), the plan's duplicate in a title attribute and the empty parts gone; ctx-atom: a cited var's tests listed by reach, not only direct callers (my.agent/tests-reaching, on jolt/a-cited-vars-tests-are-its-own-query), and the call graph followed from the cited entry to the fix site (trim-word, first-sentence, member-rank and allot went uncited); the incremental index's correctness red (r3.inc-test/incremental-equals-full) fixed; then the instructions' existing-tests pointer swapped from my.agent/callers to my.agent/tests-reaching within the 1,200-char bound (`seon.template/instructions`). Audit 3 runs as audits 1 and 2 did: a fresh worktree of main, the ten requests (`seon.fixtures/requests`) written through `seon.template/request` on its root with no model, each reproduction evaluated on main first (a request that answers right is replaced), each first-turn context read through the server's :context op, and a fresh Sonnet reviewer judging each against its request and P1-P9 with one verdict. Only a CLEAN verdict lets the paid run be proposed to root: the ten requests, thinking off, one whole task each, about $0.07-0.15 a run of ten as measured on 2026-10-26, within the $30 budget, each run's first turn checked; P8 (a hang becomes root's task) must hold too. Known problems P1-P9 (the db1 run, 1 of 10 merged; this task now holds that note): P1 the request set is checked against main (done), P2 and P3 tests and cited code on the page (done, see the-measured-requests-name-their-cause), P4 section text not addresses (done), P5 a reproduction in every request (done), P6 and P7 the template says reproduce first and build the input as the function's tests do (done, seon.template-test), P8 a hang is seen (seon.problems/stalled-agents) and P9 a refused whole task gets its verdict and main's removed tests judge a branch (seon.agent/test-plan :removed) are on main." :seon.task/kind :work :seon.task/proofs #{seon.template-test/the-measured-requests-are-a-users-with-their-reproduction}}
 {:seon.task/name "related-view-states-how-to-read-once"
  :seon.task/plan "```seon.context/related-view```'s :read and :more hint strings print first on every page, about 330 chars, ahead of the sections and functions they explain (root, from agent view's render work, 2026-10-27). Put them after the lists, or drop them from the page when the cut's own text (how much it left, the form that reads the rest) already says it, whichever leaves one statement of how to read a related item. Measure the page's tokens before and after on two of the live-agents audit's pages (audit2's first-turn contexts), and pin the order or the absence in a test."
