 {:seon.task/name "the-pages-related-part-reads-what-the-branch-changed"
  :seon.task/plan "```seon.context/relevance``` takes the context's touched items (the vars the branch changed; their mean vector is its zt feature and they seed its PageRank), and on 57 merged tasks' plans with three changed items touched it found 0.481 of the rest of the functions against 0.427 cold, 0.837 of the sections against 0.798 (docs/vector.md#relevance). ```seon.context/related``` takes them as :touched, but neither caller passes them: the page's related part (```seon.render.ui/related```) would have to diff the branch against its base in the render, which reads the whole diff and so runs again (about 100 ms on Seon's 5k texts) at every write the branch makes, and ```my.task/related``` must show what the page shows. The turn already takes that diff once (```seon.agent/branch-changes```, its readers the contract gaps, the merge's divergence and the test plan). The change: the vars the turn's diff changed are a key of ctx (written once a turn by the turn's observe, as its other turn facts), the related component and my.task/related read that key, so both are warm and the component runs again only when the changed set moves. Measure: the component's runs over a ten-turn session before and after, and the related lines a merged task's agent reads (a run's evals naming one)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/related-is-warm-with-what-the-branch-changed}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-agent-page-shows-what-the-agent-knows"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner's design for what an agent is sent and what the person sees (2026-10-28, worked out in conversation; the mock is https://claude.ai/artifact/MRzLTTwTTiTJMdtryzT9pG, which drifted and must be rebuilt from this entry, one example task carried through every part). Agreed so far:

1. ONE CONVERSATION, VERBATIM. The model reads its own past turns exactly as it wrote them (seon.model/wire-message already replays each call as sent); results come back only as tool results, never in the assistant role, never beside the code elsewhere. A cheap check: every assistant message in a request is byte-identical to what the model emitted. Compaction is deferred: get one continuous conversation of prose and forms working first.

2. THE EVAL CALL. Keep today's tool (seon.model/eval-tool): `prose` (markdown, the step's heading, written first) then `code`; a `;;` comment directly above each form is that form's description (seon.repl eval-code already reads it as :lead). Each top-level form is its own eval with its own handle, run in order, the first error stopping the rest. Live test on deepseek-flash (100 runs): verbatim history keeps the format (17/20 and 15/20 vs 8/20 when history was flattened to form->value lines); no answer shape made the model write results into its code (0/100); results-only answers were more reliable than ones echoing the code (20/20 calls vs 16/20); a `;;` line on every form ~70%; no model reused a result handle.

3. THE ANSWER. One result per form, results only (no code echoed), with its handle (result/eN), stopping at an error. OPEN, the owner's objection: the model must be able to tell which handle belongs to which form. Options to decide: the answer carries each form's `;;` description (the model's own words) with its handle; the model names each form; or another. Today the answer is only the last form's value: one answer per form is a change.

4. WHAT THE MODEL IS SENT, in order. System message: text only (instructions, the tutorial convention, the tool). First user message: the task and its context as text and EDN (plan, proofs, cited functions, their tests, users and uses, wiki sections, kept items) plus any setup forms' results as data. Then the turns. A stable prefix that only appends, so a normal turn busts no cache (today's a4 run: ~50% cached, because the request rewrites its front: a history note and older evals collapsing in place). The hiccup sent is semantic (no Tailwind classes or click handlers, which today's is full of). Every eval answer's line carries main's difference from the branch (merges since base, names both changed, would-conflict), computed, never stored.

5. STRUCTURE FIRST. The context is derived every turn from the task's plan: its cited functions (docstring, contract, source one click away), for each the functions it uses and the functions that use it (labelled \"Uses\" and \"Used by\", one-line docstrings, counts), the tests that reach them with status and first failure (proofs first, marked \"done when green\", passing ones collapsed), the citing wiki sections (heading and lead), main's difference. A thin plan shows that; the agent improves its context by citing things in the plan. Ranking only fills a small second tier, later, judged by turns to green (the owner's bar: ~90% relevant, used, fewer turns); tools are taught in the fixed system prompt, never ranked. Append-only: nothing shown leaves.

6. THE SIDEBAR AND KEPT ITEMS. `:seon.agent/sidebar`, a set of refs on the agent's entity, changed by the agent through its ctx atom (`(swap! ctx update :seon.agent/sidebar conj ref)`, a transaction on its branch). Model order: entry order (the asserting t, derived, as seon.context/first-t does); the person's order: pinned first, then last changed (presentation only; the model gets one line saying how the person's view is sorted, changed only when pins change, so no reorder diffs). Each box names the render that drew it (\"drawn by seon.render.ui/test-result\"; an agent's own: `:seon.render/with my.viz/x` on its data). Where a box's content is already in the transcript, the model's copy is the handle (\"(doc x) -> see result/e5\"); the person sees it in full. A task can carry setup forms run as the first evals; a form can keep its result.

7. PHASES. plan -> work -> finish -> done, each a schema over the task (a \"complete plan\" schema: citations resolve, a proof exists and is red, the plan says what it replaces); the instructions are rendered from m/explain of the current phase's schema, so they are derived; passed checks collapse (accretion, nothing swapped). Compaction, later: retract the earlier evals and turns from the agent's branch (history keeps them), end the process, fork a fresh agent on the same branch; process memory is scratch, so what matters is stored as facts first.

8. THE PERSON'S PAGE. User-facing: the conversation only (the person's messages; per call its prose as a heading, then per form its `;;` line and its outcome, derived from the value; a disclosure arrow loads the form and the value on demand, and opening it is a fact (:seon.eval/opened) the agent sees next turn). The input box at the bottom, growing as the person types. The task lives in the sidebar, not the transcript; what the last turn changed is highlighted, computed from the turn's EDN diff (never labels written into the model's copy). The done line is seon.reconcile/done-explain's answer: one line and a handle.

9. THE DEBUG PAGE. The request exactly as sent, in provider roles, as syntax-highlighted EDN; the person-only metadata (time, tokens, cached vs new, which render drew each part, when each entered) in a separate margin labelled as not sent; the cache point per turn, with anything that busts it labelled why; each turn's diff as the EDN it is.

OPEN beyond 3: the per-eval description when a form has no `;;` line (show its code); teaching handle reuse (one example in the instructions); text as data for reading files (`lines`-style readers, args last for ->>), decided from real runs.

Next: one example task from scratch, carried through 1-9; an independent reader with none of this history checks it for gaps; then the mock is rebuilt from it; then the build plan."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agent-reads-what-its-entity-names"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-10-30: \"I want a way to put data or a ref or a set of refs on an agents entity record and I want pull to pull in all references to that pattern ... and I want a better system for representing the levels of detail ... I want it to work for all markdown files so nothing too complex\"; and, on the design below, \"I like it\". Why: every agent should read the testing instructions (docs/testing.md#testing-in-the-world) without a copy in the help page or the prompt that drifts from them, and the same for any wiki the system wants every agent to hold.
The design (approved):
1. A reference is [address depth]. The address is a markdown path in the checkout with an optional anchor: \"docs/testing.md#testing-in-the-world\" is that section and the sections under it; \"docs/testing.md\" (no anchor) is the whole page, every section of it, those added later too (the wildcard, no glob syntax); any markdown file works (\"AGENTS.md#5-laws\", \"README.md\"). The depth is one of three keywords: :summary (the heading and its lead's first sentence), :lead (the heading, the lead and the headings below), :full (the text whole). They are today's summary, doc and code (docs/references.md#the-delimiter-picks-the-depth), renamed for what they show; prose delimiters keep working and map onto them (a bare word :summary, one backtick or a link :lead, three backticks :full). A link with no anchor shows the page whole at its depth, where today it shows only the page's first section.
2. :seon.agent/reads on the agent's entity: a set of references. The agent's ctx pull pattern (seon.facts's agent pattern) holds it, and its render resolves each through seon.doc/view and seon.ref/shown, the one renderer a task's citations already go through, so a task's citations and an agent's reads are drawn alike.
3. Defaults: one config key, :seon.config.agent/reads (first value #{[\"docs/testing.md\" :lead]}), written on every agent at its spawn; changing it changes what new agents read. An agent adds or drops its own at its REPL: (my.agent/read! address depth) and (my.agent/unread! address), recorded on its entity, held across turns.
Cost: every :full reference is paid in every request's tokens (cached, still read), so defaults stay :lead and an agent opens more with my.agent/doc or read!. A cost proof: an agent's reads cost the sections they name, drawn once a wiki change, not once a turn.
Owner: the context session (the ctx pull and its render); the optimization session proves and merges it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-status-declares-its-summary"
  :seon.task/plan "What was seen, in the lab, 2026-11-04: 22 stray red tests made the status 32k tokens a turn, repeated on the page each request. A prototype render, each red's name, its first failure and a form that reads it, made it 2k. What should be true: the status's declared model render is that summary, any agent can override it, and the whole is a read away, by a form the render names. The no-cuts ruling of 2026-10-30 is about an agent's own values: this is a render's choice with the rest a form away, and the owner's call. my.agent/red does not exist yet."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/many-red-tests-draw-a-summary-with-what-reads-the-rest}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-agents-data-makes-its-context"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-05: \"Design the agents entity with useful information and make it so the pull pattern and the render functions generate the majority of the agents context. It teaches them about the system, it explain what task they are working on, how messaging works when they get a message, evertyhing. It's all data driven.\" Design first, written here and read by the owner before anything is built.
What exists, so nothing is rebuilt: the agent's pattern, seon.ctx/pattern-of; a key of an agent's own with its render, my.schema/register! (seon.ctx-test/an-agent-adds-a-datum-and-its-render); a layout on its branch (seon.ctx-test/a-layout-declared-after-the-first-draw-is-the-page); a message's records, seon.context/message-messages. What today is not data: the system message is docs/prompt.md, the same bytes for every agent (seon.context/system-text), and seon.context/request assembles the transcript.
Citing is no special case (the owner, 2026-11-05: \"NO SPECIAL CASE FOR CITING. That's just data and a render function\"), and today it is one: seon.render.ui/page-cites walks ctx for cites and the layout places each by hand as seon.render.ui/cited, and an agent's defaults have their own config key and writer, :seon.config.agent/cites and seon.task/agent-cites-tx, called from two places, beside my.agent/cite! and my.agent/uncite!. The design says what of that is left once what an agent reads is data its pull holds and a render draws, with nothing about citing in the layout.
The owner, 2026-11-05: find all the alternative systems for generating context, and remove them or rewrite them as evals whose results as data are useful, or as data on the agent's entity drawn by render functions, which may ask for the current db: no other mechanisms.
What the first trace found at main 6ad65a13ee, all reaching the model through seon.context/request and seon.model/eval-tool. Assembly: request itself; parts-message with part-change and its hashes; page-lists choosing a DOM list; the print cache (printed-run, cache-of, drawn-at). Text not from data: docs/prompt.md through system-text; eval-tool's descriptions; session-open and the example session tags. Placed by hand: seon.render.ui/task-list naming each part, page-cites. Beside the conversation: root's stage messages (stage-boundary, seon.template) and how a kind is worked. Added to an eval's answer beside its value: again, helper-shadowed, the uncontracted finding and its fix text, main-line. Their own writers: :seon.config.agent/cites, seon.task/agent-cites-tx, my.agent/cite!, my.agent/uncite!. Second renders for the model: seon.context/status-model, seon.render.ui/task-model, seon.render.ui/cite-model, seon.render.ui/problems-model. Not traced yet: seon.learn, seon.context/relevance's reach, seon.context/help-text, the inside of every answer key.
The design as it stands, 2026-11-05, read from the code and not yet tried. The owner: pull -> ctx -> rendered hiccup -> model context, so the agent sees what the person sees; the walk re-renders by what changed, and the debug page steps through turns with the existing renders, no new render and no scaffolding. Data: a model call keeps the commit it read (:seon.model-call/basis); its :seon.model-call/sent, the :hashes and the :printed cache go. Functions, composed: a kept walk moved to a db (the shape of seon.render.proc/advance-walk!: seon.ctx/moved, seon.ctx/patch, the atom's reset, seon.render/flush! with the moved datoms, so the ctx paths and the db reads each reach only what they read), the walk's hiccup (seon.render/resolved), and one function from hiccup to messages, its text kept on the component's cache entry by its revision as :unit-html is. A turn is the walk at its head, then messages. The page is the walk, then html. The debug page at turn n is a walk moved to call n's commit, then both. Goes: seon.context/request, parts-of, drawn-at, parts-message, part-change, printed-run, cache-of, wire-turn, seon.render.ui/debug-input, debug-turn, ctx-parts, the :only draw. Proofs: stepping a scripted session reproduces each recorded request byte for byte; a step costs what changed between its commits, flat in the session. Tried at a REPL on 6ad65a13ee, 2026-11-05, on synthetic sessions of one eval a turn, reads and writes both counted on the kept walk; nothing merged. A walk moves any way: 17 hops forward, back and across, its units and ctx equal to a cold build's at each. Drawing every list as it is costs n squared, because seon.render.ui/chat-turn calls seon.render.ui/turn-evals, which rebuilds and sorts the whole conversation for every model call it draws: round trip 31, 272, 3719 MB at 10, 50, 200 records. Entities hold the data (the owner, 2026-11-05: \"There are no rows ... An agent has turns, it has evals, it's just data\"; \"Nothing should require N^2 because we are progressively building things\"). What a turn needs is the records holding its number: the conversation is a component per turn number, each reading its records from the index by that number (:seon.eval/turn and :seon.message/turn need :seon.db/index, or an AVET read answers empty with no error; d/with-reads then records exactly [:av a v], so a record of another turn touches nothing it read), chat-turn given its evals, and seon.render.ui/task-list, seon.render.ui/run-head and seon.render.ui/tiles reading the agent's turn and :seon.eval/schema instead of walking every eval. Where the calls come from: of 2073 contracted calls in one step at 40 turns, 8 components drew; the rest is the walk's own work per visit (seon.ratom/view?, seon.render/inputs-in, seon.render/child-key, seon.ratom/view=). Two causes, both tried: seon.render/flush! matches every datom against every db reader and seon.render/render! matches them again on every visit (22 KB a reader a step), where an index from read key to readers matches them once, as seon.ratom's does for ctx paths; and the list visited every earlier turn to learn nothing moved, where emit can keep the marker of a form it has drawn. One step forward, before and after: 1623, 2073, 3873, 11073 calls and 7.3, 10.7, 24.6, 80.9 MB against 1387, 1447, 1687, 2647 calls and 6.4, 7.6, 12.9, 34.6 MB at 10, 40, 160, 640 turns, equal to a cold build at each of 17 hops. seon.ctx/moved is 4.65 MB of that, a diff only stepping pays; a live turn holds its transaction's datoms. Earlier numbers on this entry's first trial numbered turns from 100, which drew about 100 empty components, so their fixed part is too high. Still growing with the session, to find: about 45 KB a turn, in part seon.ctx/members reading a whole collection when one member joined. The wire resends the conversation every request whatever we do: that is the API, and the text is kept, not made again. Printing the hiccup for the model costs far too much: 1066 tokens a turn, 307 with classes stripped, against 40 for that turn's content on the wire. Writing it as the text a person reads costs what the wire does: 714 tokens against 666 over 12 turns. So the last step is hiccup to text, not hiccup printed. Still to try: a value's text comes out as :step5, where seon.print/text gives {:step 5, :ok true}, so a value should stay a call of the printer in the hiccup that each writer expands; roles, tool calls and a reply's exact bytes on the wire, including Markdown drawn from its source; the recorded requests compared byte for byte; run-head's :changed, dropped in the trial, is the owner's call; an eval with no :seon.eval/turn draws nothing in its turn's component (production writes it on all but the loop's own eval, which is not drawn; fixtures must too); an agent-built page in a value's own :seon.render/html is missed by reading :seon.eval/schema; real sessions, whose evals outnumber their turns. The debug page draws history with today's renders and keeps a hash of the bytes sent on each model call, to say when its redraw differs (the owner, 2026-11-05: yes).
Corrected by the owner, 2026-11-05, standing over the design above where they differ. The hiccup stays hiccup: lighter, with its CSS out, and each piece naming its render, as seon.render/part-form on jolt/render-core does, `[seon.render.ui/status {:ctx path} ...]`. The context is @ctx's records by their own model renders (roles, call ids and exact bytes are in @ctx) plus the rendered hiccup. The render is an ordinary eval at turn 0, as (my.render/render-ctx) on jolt/render-core, and when @ctx or the hiccup changes another eval answers the diffs, as (my.render/render-changes), which the agent sees on its next turn; so the context accumulates, the prefix stays byte-identical, and the debug page steps through what is recorded. No page as the last message. Tried at a REPL: messages from @ctx's records equal seon.context/request's byte for byte (49 messages, synthetic), the named light form 863 tokens against 720. Large or changing output is the render's bug. Stepped through six live sessions of today's new schema (store of .worktrees/live-r2, cloned): first page 3615-4947 tokens; every change sent whole 55066 tokens over 192 steps: status 34853, run-head 11707 (it changed on all 192, for :tokens-sent and a growing :changed), cited 5495, task-data 2260, namespaces 751. Causes: a failing proof's status holds a printed exception (913 tokens) and the test the agent wrote (322); task-data sends the plan again when :to-prove changes, which status already says; cited sends a var's source back after the agent's own edit, with tests, callers and reaching; namespaces lists clojure.core's names. A trial filter over those: first page 2969-4023, changes 18099 (status about 11176, run-head 4936, cited 1319, namespaces 668, task-data 0). The filter drops a thrown error's message, which the real render must keep (895ea0282f on jolt/render-core), so those are low. Not tried: the evals themselves, in a session; whether a model given them goes quiet (an opening of inserted evals had 0 of 6 replies with text on one shape); turns to green. Names to agree with the owner: component for part, unit and tile; agent-view; agent-messages; render tree for walk.
Questions the design answers, each with a measurement from a real request: which attributes of :seon.db/agent-fact answer a question the agent has, and which are bookkeeping; what of docs/prompt.md moves to data an agent's pull holds, given that a system message is cached and the page, once it is the last message, is not; how a message's own render says how messaging works when one arrives; what jolt/render-core's renders already do of this.
Owner: the context session."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-context-is-records"
  :seon.task/plan "What is left of it. The context is records in turn order and request's page message, sent refs and hashes are gone (2026-11-06, jolt/render-evals). Still in src from the list it was to delete, each kept while its reader needs it: seon.context/wire-turn (the debug page's redraw), seon.context/drawn-parts (a read with no live walk: the judge's first move, a debug redraw), seon.context/page-lists and seon.render/placed-hiccup, placed-data, draw-all and :only (the walk draws the task's list alone until a person's page asks for the rest), seon.render.ui/debug-input, debug-turn and ctx-parts (the debug page draws by these rather than by the conversation's own renders), seon.render.ui/problems-model. To do: draw the debug page with the conversation's own renders and delete what only it reads; decide :only and draw-all against what an unwatched walk saves, by a measure. seon.render.proc/pass-view's debug read makes its request cold, 317 calls a record (seon.render-cost-test/drawing-latest-costs-one-request): pass it an earlier :printed, as a turn does."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}

 {:seon.task/name "a-request-costs-its-new-records"
  :seon.task/plan "A request prints only its new records (seon.context/request's :printed), and it still reads and compares every record of ctx: 969 calls and 0.71 MB at 10 evals, 8 calls and 15 KB an eval (seon.context-cost-test/a-requests-cost-is-its-records), so linear, where the plan asked flat. Accepted for now (2026-11-06): the wire body holds n messages anyway, and 8 calls an eval is small at a 40-turn cap. Correct if a cap grows or a measure says so: a request takes the records since the last it held, by @ctx's order, and notices an unlinked one by its ctx change, not by a comparison each."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-cost-test/a-requests-cost-is-its-records}}

 {:seon.task/name "teaching-is-data"
  :seon.task/plan "An agent writes no prose: 0 of 9 replies held text on the merged reconcile run (2026-11-06, render-evals), though the system message asks for it and seon.render/the-render-is-an-eval's plan asked a live session to still write text. The person's chat reads nothing but evals; what teaches it, and does the first eval being Seon's suppress it? A third real run (2026-11-06, reconcile on render-evals): the proof written at turn 5 and three functions fixed, then the same form 17 times from turn 24 to its cap, each answered by :again. An identical repeat is the biggest loss measured. A second real run (2026-11-06, reconcile on render-evals): the fix written by turn 7 and every proof green at turn 9, then 31 turns reading seon.cost because the status said `red test that is no proof`, a test the agent could not act on. A red that is not the agent's says whose it is and what the agent does: message root, or stop reading. A real run to answer (2026-11-05, long-string on render-speaks, cap 40, 693k tokens): 39 evals of reading, one `source` a turn, no proof written, the last five the same form, each told by :again to run something else. The status names the proof to write from the first request. What in the page makes an agent write the proof and try something before it has read everything? The owner, 2026-11-05: the agent's entity \"teaches them about the system, it explain what task they are working on, how messaging works when they get a message, evertyhing. It's all data driven\"; \"NO SPECIAL CASE FOR CITING\"; \"NO OTHER MECHANISMS\". What an agent reads is an eval whose result is useful or data on its entity drawn by a render. docs/prompt.md moves to data an agent's pull holds, drawn by a render, and evals at its opening, with seon.model/eval-tool's descriptions reduced to its shape; session-open and the example session tags go; a message's render says how messaging works; root's stage messages and how a kind is worked become data. Citing has no writer of its own: seon.render.ui/page-cites, seon.task/agent-cites-tx, :seon.config.agent/cites, my.agent/cite! and uncite! go for ordinary data and swap!. An eval's answer holds its value: :again, :helper-shadowed, the uncontracted finding and its fix, and main-line become data on the agent, drawn, or go. Also traces what the first pass left: seon.learn, seon.context/relevance, seon.context/help-text. Agents' own renders are taught here: a key of its own and a render (docs/ctx.md#adding-a-datum-and-its-render). Proofs: two agents with different data read different context with no code changed; the words of the wire's system message come from the entity."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
